<Base title="What Is Fungal Research?" description="Discover the many branches of modern mycology, independent community science projects, and ways to contribute to real fungal research.">
<section class="page-hero container"><p class="eyebrow">INVESTIGATE / FIND YOUR WAY IN</p><h1>What is fungal research?</h1><p class="lead">It's much bigger than identifying mushrooms. It's the ongoing effort to understand fungal life—and there are more ways to take part than you might imagine.</p><p><a class="button primary" href="#fit">Find your kind of question ↓</a></p></section>
<section class="section soft story-with-photo"><div class="container narrative-split"><div class="narrative-copy"><p class="eyebrow">THE BIG PICTURE</p><h2>A kingdom we have barely begun to document.</h2><p>Fungi shape ecosystems, agriculture, medicine, food webs and the evolution of life. Yet much of their diversity remains undescribed, and many habitats are poorly sampled. Modern mycology spans field biology, taxonomy, evolutionary genetics, microscopy, physiology, ecology, conservation, genomics and more.</p><p>The challenge is not simply that fungi are hard to see. Research and conservation also face shortages of specialists, collections infrastructure, training opportunities and funding. A 2025 scientific declaration highlights these constraints; a 2024 review specifically argues that citizen science can help address major fungal conservation data gaps.</p><p><strong>An important distinction:</strong> evidence supports chronic underinvestment and taxonomic neglect, but not a universal claim that all mycology funding is currently declining. Molecular research and fungal conservation initiatives are growing in some areas even as traditional taxonomy and documentation remain stretched.</p><div class="research-reading"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/conl.13013" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read: The power of citizen science to advance fungal conservation (2024) ↗</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/ppp3.10569" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read: The 2030 Declaration on Scientific Plant and Fungal Collecting (2025) ↗</a></div></div><figure class="narrative-photo"><img src="/images/field-photography/learning-man-2.jpg" alt="Naturalists comparing observations during a field learning session" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption>ORIGINAL FIELD PHOTOGRAPHY · · Replace with a photograph illustrating fungal biodiversity</figcaption></figure></div></section>
<section class="section" id="ways"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">MODERN MYCOLOGY</p><h2>Six kinds of questions you could help answer.</h2><p class="resource-intro">These are overlapping research activities, not exclusive career tracks. You can start with an observation and end up collaborating on a species description—or contribute valuable data without ever publishing a paper.</p><p class="mine-legend">Some research fields link to a concrete example of my work below. Other examples will be added as those stories develop.</p><div class="fungal-fields">{areas.map(a=><article class={a.example?'fungal-field is-mine':'fungal-field'}><div class="fungal-field-top"><span class="fungal-field-number">{a.number}</span>{a.example&&<a class="mine-badge" href={a.example} aria-label={`See my work in ${a.title}`}><Image src={logo} alt="" width={18} densities={[1,2]} format="webp" />See my work ↓</a>}</div><h3>{a.title}</h3><p class="fungal-field-question">{a.question}</p><p><strong>What you can do:</strong> {a.action}</p><p class="fungal-field-next">{a.lead} {a.links.map(([label,href],i)=><>{i>0&&(i===a.links.length-1?' and ':', ')}<a href={href} {...(href.startsWith('http')?{target:'_blank',rel:'noopener noreferrer'}:{})}>{label}</a></>)}{a.tail&&` ${a.tail}`}{a.links.length>0&&!a.tail&&'.'}</p></article>)}</div><p class="resource-footnote">Looking for a more precise distinction between taxonomy, systematics, phylogenetics and nomenclature? <a href="/investigate/resources/">Read the discipline guide →</a></p></div></section>
<section class="section soft story-with-photo"><div class="container narrative-split"><div class="narrative-copy"><p class="eyebrow">WHY COMMUNITY SCIENCE MATTERS</p><h2>More eyes, more places, more questions.</h2><p>Professional researchers cannot survey every forest, examine every collection or monitor every season. Community scientists can contribute geographic reach, repeat observations, local ecological knowledge, collections and specialized skills. Research partnerships can turn that work into data useful for taxonomy, conservation and ecology.</p><p>But participation is not a substitute for rigor. Useful observations need context; sequence matches need verification; conservation claims need defensible methods. Nor should volunteer enthusiasm be used as an excuse to underfund professional scientists, collections and laboratories. The strongest work is collaborative—and gives people appropriate training, access and credit.</p></div><figure class="narrative-photo"><img src="/images/field-photography/myco-friends-1.jpg" alt="Mushroom enthusiasts sharing a field experience" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption>IMAGE PLACEHOLDER · Replace with your own collaboration or foray photograph</figcaption></figure></div></section>
<section class="section" id="projects"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">PEOPLE BUILDING NEW PATHWAYS</p><h2>Community science is already happening.</h2><p class="resource-intro">These are examples of independent and collaborative initiatives—not an exhaustive ranking, and not all open to every volunteer at all times.</p><div class="fungal-initiatives">{initiatives.map((p,i)=><article class="fungal-initiative"><div class="fungal-person">{people[i].photo?<img src={people[i].photo} alt={`Portrait of ${people[i].names}`} loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" />:<a class="fungal-photo-pending" href={people[i].profile} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label={`View official photographs and team information for ${people[i].names}`}><span aria-hidden="true">◎</span><small>View the people behind the project ↗</small></a>}<div class="fungal-person-caption"><strong>{people[i].names}</strong><span>{people[i].role}</span>{people[i].photoCredit&&<small>{people[i].photoCredit}</small>}</div></div><p class="eyebrow">{p.tag}</p><h3>{p.name}</h3><p class="fungal-field-question">{p.focus}</p><p>{p.description}</p><p><strong>Where you might fit:</strong> {p.participate}</p><a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{p.link} ↗</a></article>)}</div><p class="resource-footnote">Photographs shown here are editorial mockups for review, not yet licensed for republication. Credits identify the source, not permission. For projects without a confidently identified photograph, the card links to the project team rather than showing unrelated people.</p></div></section>
<section class="section closer" id="closer"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">CLOSER TO HOME</p><h2>What this looks like for me.</h2><div class="narrative-split personal-intro"><div class="resource-intro narrative-copy"><p>I got my start with mushrooms as a cultivator. There was something fascinating about watching them grow, learning what they needed, and figuring out how to give them the right conditions. The more time I spent growing mushrooms, the more I wanted to understand them.</p><p>Eventually, that curiosity took me beyond cultivation and into the woods around my home. I quickly realized that the handful of species we commonly cultivate barely scratches the surface of the incredible diversity of fungi growing all around us. I was hooked. I wanted to find them all.</p><p>iNaturalist became my way of keeping track of what I was finding. I could photograph a mushroom, document where and when I found it, and come back to that observation later when I wanted to learn more. I don't think I fully appreciated just how much it would change the way I approached mycology, but I immediately recognized how powerful a tool it could be.</p><p>What started with growing mushrooms eventually became a much bigger adventure in learning about them. And the more I learn, the more questions I seem to have.</p></div><figure class="narrative-photo"><img src="/images/field-photography/learning-man-1.jpg" alt="Naturalist documenting a fungal collection with field equipment" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption>IMAGE PLACEHOLDER · Replace with a personal fieldwork portrait or research photograph</figcaption></figure></div><ol class="closer-steps">{story.map(c=><li class="closer-step" id={c.title==='From observations to sequences on iNaturalist'?'my-inat':c.title==='Amateur Mycology Publishing Group'?'ampg':undefined}>{c.draft&&<span class="draft-flag">Draft</span>}<div class="story-card-photo" role="img" aria-label="Photograph placeholder for this personal research story"><span>YOUR PHOTO HERE</span><small>{c.tag === "My path" ? "iNaturalist / fieldwork" : c.tag === "Publishing" ? "Publishing group / collaborators" : "NEO MYCO / field survey"}</small></div><p class="eyebrow">{c.tag}</p><h3>{c.title}</h3><p class="closer-status">{c.status}</p>{c.body.map(t=><p>{t}</p>)}{c.tag==='My path'&&<button type="button" class="story-open" data-story-open aria-haspopup="dialog">Read my story ↗</button>}{c.tag==='Publishing'&&<button type="button" class="story-open" data-ampg-open aria-haspopup="dialog">Read the story ↗</button>}{c.title==='NEO MYCO'&&<button type="button" class="story-open" data-neo-open aria-haspopup="dialog">Learn about NEO MYCO ↗</button>}{'youtubeId' in c&&(c.youtubeId?<div class="closer-video"><iframe src={`https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/${c.youtubeId}`} title={`${c.title} video`} loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>:<div class="closer-video is-pending" aria-hidden="true"><span>▶</span><small>Video coming soon</small></div>)}{c.link&&<a href={c.link[1]} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{c.link[0]} ↗</a>}</li>)}</ol><dialog class="story-dialog" id="inat-story-dialog" aria-labelledby="inat-story-title"><div class="story-dialog-inner"><button type="button" class="story-close" data-story-close aria-label="Close story">×</button><p class="eyebrow">MY PATH</p><h2 id="inat-story-title">From observations to sequences</h2><p>Back when I met and became friends with Kyle Canan of Ohio Mushroom DNA Lab, he was doing nanopore DNA sequencing right out of his home. I started sending him interesting mushrooms I'd collected, and before long, it became a regular thing.</p><p>The very first mushroom I had sequenced was <em>Amanita</em> sp. 'IN10', which, funny enough, I kept finding in my own front yard among white oaks. After seeing it return year after year, I started getting curious about whether it was actually an already-described species or something that still needed to be formally named.</p><p>Later, my friend Ashley invited me to a Learning Man gathering, where I met Amanita expert Melissa Silva. Melissa had already been investigating IN10 and believed it was likely <em>Amanita velatipes</em>. We talked about working toward formally resolving that provisional name, although the project is on hold for now.</p><p>It's a good example of how mushroom hunting, DNA sequencing, and friendships can lead to some pretty interesting scientific questions, sometimes right in your own front yard.</p><a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/people/mycogeekyohio" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore my iNaturalist observations ↗</a></div></dialog><dialog class="story-dialog" id="ampg-story-dialog" aria-labelledby="ampg-story-title"><div class="story-dialog-inner"><button type="button" class="story-close" data-ampg-close aria-label="Close story">×</button><p class="eyebrow">PUBLISHING TOGETHER</p><h2 id="ampg-story-title">Amateur Mycology Publishing Group</h2><h3>How it started</h3><p>Scott Ostuni and Kyle Canan began working together to investigate potentially novel species from the growing list of provisional taxa emerging from Kyle’s sequencing work at Ohio Mushroom DNA Lab. They wanted to understand which might need formal descriptions and which could correspond to species already described.</p><p>They began receiving mentorship from mycologists including Joshua Birkebak and Alfredo Justo. As the provisional list continued to grow, Scott recognized the scale of the challenge and created what we now casually call the Amateur Mycology Publishing Group. From around 80 applicants, he selected a small cohort of enthusiastic amateur mycologists who wanted to learn how to participate in this work.</p><h3>What it meant for me</h3><p>We meet on Zoom every two weeks. People bring different levels of experience, but we're all learning the intricate process of taxonomic research, from evaluating provisional taxa to understanding what it takes to formally describe species.</p><p>Being included was a generous and rare opportunity. Learning alongside bright, motivated people and receiving guidance from experienced mycologists has helped me enormously in making sense of this work. It's played a huge role in shaping my own path in amateur mycology.</p><p>It's also a big part of why The MycoView and this new MycoGeeky website exist. I want to help create more opportunities for curious people to learn together, build relationships, and contribute to science.</p><div class="ampg-video-placeholder"><strong>Coming to The MycoView</strong><p>A video telling this story will be linked here when it's ready.</p></div></div></dialog><dialog class="story-dialog" id="neo-story-dialog" aria-labelledby="neo-story-title"><div class="story-dialog-inner"><button type="button" class="story-close" data-neo-close aria-label="Close story">×</button><p class="eyebrow">COMING IN 2027</p><h2 id="neo-story-title">NEO MYCO</h2><p>NEO MYCO—the Northeast Ohio Mycology Collaborative—is a new community we're building around a simple idea: you don't have to be a scientist to do science.</p><h3>Why we're building it</h3><p>Some people want to learn the names of the mushrooms in their backyard. Others want to spend more time in the woods, meet people who share their curiosity, or learn how to document fungi in ways that can actually help researchers. I think a mycology community should make room for all of that.</p><p>My own journey has been shaped by people willing to share what they know and invite others into the work. NEO MYCO is an opportunity to help create more of those connections here in Northeast Ohio.</p><h3>What we're working toward</h3><p>Our guiding ideas are <strong>Explore • Learn • Document • Contribute</strong>. We're developing plans for field outings, practical learning, careful iNaturalist documentation, and opportunities to participate in community science. One idea we're exploring is <em>Know Your Woods</em>, helping people return to familiar places and learn what grows there over time.</p><p>More advanced opportunities could include specimen documentation, DNA barcoding, and research mentorship. These are goals we're working toward, not a promise that every program is already running.</p><div class="ampg-video-placeholder"><strong>NEO MYCO website coming soon</strong><p>When the club's website is ready, we'll link it here so you can explore programs, events, and ways to get involved.</p></div></div></dialog><div class="survey-block" id="surveys"><div class="survey-intro"><p class="eyebrow">LONG-TERM SURVEYS</p><h3>Watching the same places, year after year.</h3><p>A forest never reveals all its fungi in a single visit. I return to familiar places to document what appears across seasons and years, and I contribute to collaborative surveys that turn observations into lasting evidence.</p></div><div class="survey-project-grid"><article class="survey-project-card"><p class="eyebrow">COLLABORATIVE SURVEY</p><h4>Crane Hollow Nature Preserve</h4><p>Contributing to an ongoing fungal diversity inventory through repeated observations and research collaboration.</p><details><summary>Learn about this survey</summary><p>Crane Hollow is a collaborative biodiversity inventory. Repeated observations, field documentation, and DNA sequencing can help investigate fungal diversity and unresolved identifications.</p><a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/projects/crane-hollow-fungal-diversity-survey" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the project ↗</a></details></article><article class="survey-project-card"><p class="eyebrow">LONG-TERM INVENTORY</p><h4>Hinckley Reservation</h4><p>Building a baseline of fungal diversity through seasonal documentation and repeat visits.</p><details><summary>Learn about this survey</summary><p>Repeat seasonal observations build a baseline of fungal diversity, fruiting patterns, and habitat associations. A dedicated iNaturalist dashboard can follow.</p></details></article><article class="survey-project-card"><p class="eyebrow">RESPONSIBLE RESEARCH</p><h4>Permits &amp; partnerships</h4><p>Working with land managers so scientific collecting is authorized, careful, and useful.</p><details><summary>Why permissions matter</summary><p>Collecting permissions involving Cuyahoga Valley National Park, Cleveland Metroparks, and Summit Metro Parks make carefully documented survey work possible within each agency’s rules. Vouchers and associated records help make collections useful beyond a single visit.</p></details></article></div><SurveyDashboard client:visible /><div class="survey-why"><p class="eyebrow">WHY IT MATTERS</p><dl>{surveyWhy.map(w=><><dt>{w.title}</dt><dd>{w.text}</dd></>)}</dl></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section soft" id="fit"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">WHERE DO YOU FIT?</p><h2>Start with what you already enjoy.</h2><p class="resource-intro">You don't need to choose a title. Choose a question or activity worth returning to.</p><div class="fungal-paths">{paths.map(p=><details><summary>{p.title}</summary><p><strong>Try this first:</strong> {p.start}</p><p><strong>Go further:</strong> {p.grow}</p></details>)}</div><p class="research-invitation">A useful scientific contribution might be a carefully documented mushroom, a corrected record, a specimen someone can reexamine, a question that changes a study—or a collaboration you helped make possible.</p><div class="fungal-cta"><a class="button primary" href="/investigate/resources/">Explore research resources →</a><a class="button" href="/investigate/tools/">Open the Research Workbench →</a></div></div></section>
<script>
  const dialog = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('#inat-story-dialog');
  const openButton = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-story-open]');
  const closeButton = dialog?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-story-close]');
  openButton?.addEventListener('click', () => dialog?.showModal());
  closeButton?.addEventListener('click', () => dialog?.close());
  dialog?.addEventListener('click', (event) => { if (event.target === dialog) dialog.close(); });
  const ampgDialog = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('#ampg-story-dialog');
  document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-ampg-open]')?.addEventListener('click', () => ampgDialog?.showModal());
  ampgDialog?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-ampg-close]')?.addEventListener('click', () => ampgDialog.close());
  ampgDialog?.addEventListener('click', event => { if (event.target === ampgDialog) ampgDialog.close(); });
  const neoDialog = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('#neo-story-dialog');
  document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-neo-open]')?.addEventListener('click', () => neoDialog?.showModal());
  neoDialog?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-neo-close]')?.addEventListener('click', () => neoDialog.close());
  neoDialog?.addEventListener('click', event => { if (event.target === neoDialog) neoDialog.close(); });
</script>
